
This directory contains a variety of test infrastructure as well as some of the
tests in IvorySQL. Not all tests are here -- in particular, there are more in
individual contrib/ modules and in src/bin.

Not all these tests get run by "make oracle-check". Check src/oracle_test/Makefile to see
which tests get run automatically.

authentication/
  Tests for authentication (but see also below)

examples/
  Demonstration programs for libpq that double as regression tests via
  "make oracle-check"

isolation/
  Tests for concurrent behavior at the SQL level

kerberos/
  Tests for Kerberos/GSSAPI authentication and encryption

ldap/
  Tests for LDAP-based authentication

locale/
  Sanity checks for locale data, encodings, etc

mb/
  Tests for multibyte encoding (UTF-8) support

modules/
  Extensions used only or mainly for test purposes, generally not suitable
  for installing in production databases

perl/
  Infrastructure for Perl-based TAP tests

recovery/
  Test suite for recovery and replication

regress/
  IvorySQL-2.0's main regression test suite, pg_regress

ssl/
  Tests to exercise and verify SSL certificate handling

subscription/
  Tests for logical replication

Oracle-compatible regression notes
==================================

The tests under regress/ and modules/ are a parallel copy of src/test that is
run against a server started in Oracle-compatible mode, so their SQL and
expected files are maintained by hand and have to be kept in step with their
src/test counterparts.  Two things are easy to get wrong.

The temporary instance uses two listeners
-----------------------------------------

Both Oracle-compatible drivers start the temporary server as

    postgres -p <port> -o <ivorysql.port>

so two listeners are opened: the regular PostgreSQL port and the port named by
the ivorysql.port GUC (1521 by default).  Both ports have to be free before the
postmaster is spawned; otherwise it exits while binding the second listener and
the run stops with "postmaster failed" before a single test has run.  The
drivers therefore search for a free primary port and then for a free secondary
port.  When the suite dies right at startup, look at
tmp_check/log/postmaster.log first: it is usually an address already in use.

Keeping expected output in sync
-------------------------------

pg_regress runs psql with -a (echo all), so psql copies every line of a test's
.sql file into results/ and pg_regress compares that with expected/.  Only
blank lines and the data of a COPY ... FROM stdin block are held back;
backslash meta-commands and the lone "/" lines that Oracle mode uses to
terminate a PL/SQL block are echoed as well.  A line that a .sql file gained,
lost or fixed a typo in therefore has to be mirrored in expected/.  When a
test is ported or a .sql file is edited, update expected/ from the .sql; never
edit only one side.

Drift can be checked without a build
------------------------------------

src/oracle_test/tools/check_expected_echo.py rebuilds the lines psql is
supposed to echo for every test that a normal run executes and verifies that
one of the expected/*.out variants still contains them in order.  It needs
neither a build nor a server, so it can be run right after a sync from src/test
or from PostgreSQL to spot drift before the suite is started:

    python3 src/oracle_test/tools/check_expected_echo.py

The command exits non-zero when it finds drift and prints the offending lines.
Its unit tests run with:

    python3 src/oracle_test/tools/test_check_expected_echo.py

Module tests are limited to the directories that
src/oracle_test/modules/Makefile lists, because a directory the build never
descends into produces no results file and its drift cannot break a run.
Modules added by a conditional SUBDIRS += (openssl, C++, injection points,
LDAP, and so on) depend on how the tree was configured, so the checker cannot
tell whether they run; it treats them like tests that no schedule runs.  A test
that a normal run executes but that has no expected/*.out file at all is always
a failure, since pg_regress has nothing to compare the results with.

Pass --all to include the tests that no schedule runs and the modules that the
unconditional build list leaves out.  A few of those are left stale on purpose
(tests that Oracle mode does not execute), so their drift is reported but is
not a failure of the tree.

The checker only knows the psql echo rule; it cannot replace a real run of the
suite.  It follows \set ECHO none/all, but skips tests that use \set ECHO
queries or \set ECHO errors, or that toggle ECHO inside a conditional branch,
because in those cases the lines that reach results/ cannot be determined
without running the script.  Tests that use \if, \echo or \gexec are still
checked: psql echoes those input lines before it acts on them, so the
generated output does not affect the comparison.
